{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1ba437929b8a4b13ae23cfe9d96c6f56","ordinal":41,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.41","text":"\n\nFirst, the ways they come into being. Some animals are generated without sexual union, others from intercourse. Of those generated without union, some come from fire, like the tiny creatures that appear in furnaces; some from stagnant water, like gnats; some from wine turning sour, like midges; some from earth, ⟨like ...⟩; some from slime, like frogs; some from mud, like worms; some from donkeys, like beetles; some from vegetables, like caterpillars; some from fruit, like the gall-insects that come from wild figs; and some from rotting animals, like bees from bulls and wasps from horses."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3e4a80ee161e435d9145dad0024124f3","ordinal":42,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.42","text":"\n\nOf those generated from intercourse, most come from parents of the same kind, but some from parents of different kinds, like mules. Again, in general, some animals are born live, like human beings; some are born as eggs, like birds; and some are born as lumps of flesh, like bears.\n〔1〕 It was widely believed in antiquity that bear cubs are born as shapeless lumps of flesh that the mother licks into shape."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d23ee669f16246dd8beb7970f199ac55","ordinal":43,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.43","text":"\n\nIt is likely, then, that these dissimilarities and differences in the way they come into being produce great differences in how they are affected, and that from this source they derive their incompatibility, their lack of harmony and their conflict."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-66f29b7ca7c1481fa05db0011e271aa4","ordinal":44,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.44","text":"\n\nMoreover, differences in the most important parts of the body, especially those whose nature is to judge and to perceive, can produce a very great conflict among impressions [owing to the variation among animals]. People with jaundice, at any rate, say that things which appear white to us are yellow, and people with a bloodshot eye say they are blood-red. Now, some animals have yellow eyes, others bloodshot, others whitish, others some other color. So it is likely, I think, that they perceive colors differently."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-12024b86448b4a1aa12115a660724f28","ordinal":45,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.45","text":"\n\nAgain, if we stare at the sun for a long time and then bend over a book, the letters seem to us golden and moving around. Now, some animals naturally have a brightness in their eyes and send out from them a fine and mobile light, so that they can even see at night. So we would be right to think that external things do not strike them and us in the same way."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-83fb28be9fb2428299401c5101b558ff","ordinal":46,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.46","text":"\n\nConjurers, too, by smearing lamp-wicks with copper rust or cuttlefish ink, make the people present look copper-colored at one moment and black at another, just by sprinkling in a little of the mixture. Surely, then, it is far more reasonable that, since different humors are blended into the vision of ⟨animals⟩, different"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ae99052ebdf8468bba7e4bec7c3e5ecd","ordinal":47,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.47","text":"\n\nimpressions should arise for them. When we press the side of an eye, the outlines, shapes and sizes of visible things appear elongated and narrow. So it is likely that animals whose pupils are slanting and elongated—goats, cats and the like—have impressions of the underlying things as different, and do not suppose them to be such as animals with round pupils do."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4ecf94e49c954824be426ac15b9213ea","ordinal":48,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.48","text":"\n\nMirrors, too, because of their different construction, sometimes show external objects as very small, as concave mirrors do, and sometimes as elongated and narrow, as convex ones do. Some show the head of the person looking into them at the bottom and the feet at the top."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-625c48570131424ab3f826e33e56f3e6","ordinal":49,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.49","text":"\n\nNow, of the organs of sight, some bulge right out because of their convexity, some are more concave, and some lie flat. So it is likely that for this reason too impressions are altered, and that dogs, fish, lions and human beings do not see the same things as equal in size or alike in shape, but each according to the imprint made by the vision that receives the appearance."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-316f1d48784e48ccbbe4a0e27ae47d4e","ordinal":50,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.50","text":"\n\nThe same argument holds for the other senses too. How could one say that creatures with shells, creatures with bare flesh, creatures covered in spines and creatures covered in feathers or scales are all moved in the same way by touch? How could creatures with a very narrow auditory passage and those with a very wide one perceive in the same way by hearing, or creatures with hairy ears and those with smooth ones? After all, even we are affected one way in hearing when we have stopped up our ears, and another way when we simply use them."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-dc81af070399461992449dd639beacfc","ordinal":51,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.51","text":"\n\nSmell, too, would differ with the variety of animals. We ourselves are affected one way when we have a chill and there is an excess of phlegm in us, and another way when the parts around our head take in an excess of blood: then we turn away from what seems fragrant to others and think we are, as it were, being struck by it. Now, some animals are by nature flabby and phlegmatic, others very full of blood, and in others yellow or black bile predominates and is in excess. So it is reasonable that for this reason too objects of smell should appear different to each of them."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f80d8cac46d1411ab95869fa9de258cd","ordinal":52,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.52","text":"\n\nSo too with objects of taste, since some animals have a rough, dry tongue and others a very moist one. When our own tongue becomes drier in fevers, we think what we are given is earthy and bad-tasting, or bitter; and we experience this because of the different predominance of the humors said to be in us. Since, then, animals too have different organs of taste, with different humors in excess, they would also get different impressions of the underlying things by taste."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b1cf1770cd5c4ae5bc1fd54ebfce074c","ordinal":53,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.53","text":"\n\nConsider the same food: when distributed through the body, it becomes a vein in one place, an artery in another, a bone in another, a sinew in another, and so on with each of the rest, displaying a different power according to the difference of the parts that receive it. Consider water, one and uniform: when distributed into trees, it becomes bark in one place, a branch in another, fruit in another—a fig, indeed, or a pomegranate, and so on with each of the rest."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-61e48730385a42e0a9956ec92d7c9b2e","ordinal":54,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.54","text":"\n\nConsider the musician’s breath, one and the same: blown into the pipe, it becomes high in one place and low in another; and the same pressure of the hand on the lyre produces a low note in one place and a high one in another. In just the same way, it is likely that external objects are observed as different according to the different constitutions of the animals that receive the impressions."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7aa6b107273d4e08a8f9ffca5c4ec6fc","ordinal":55,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.55","text":"\n\nOne can learn this more clearly from what animals choose and avoid. Perfume, for instance, appears very pleasant to human beings but unbearable to beetles and bees. Olive oil is beneficial to human beings, but when sprinkled on wasps and bees it kills them. Seawater is unpleasant and poisonous for human beings to drink, but very pleasant and drinkable for fish."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9b09823b737b4383a95facf040759f2e","ordinal":56,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.56","text":"\n\nPigs would rather wash in the foulest-smelling mud than in clear, clean water. Some animals eat grass, some shrubs, some woodland growth, some seeds, some flesh, some milk. Some enjoy stale food, others fresh; some raw food, others food prepared by cooking. And in general, what is pleasant to some is to others unpleasant, to be avoided, and deadly."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5171507f3c7848808792a2a25afaed57","ordinal":57,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.57","text":"\n\nHemlock, for instance, fattens quails, and henbane fattens pigs—and pigs also enjoy eating salamanders, just as deer enjoy venomous creatures and swallows blister beetles. Ants and midges, when swallowed, give human beings nausea and colic; but a bear, if she falls ill, licks them up and gets her strength back."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-eed63be357934fa99c160524d1987045","ordinal":58,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.58","text":"\n\nA viper is stupefied if merely touched by an oak branch, and so is a bat by a plane leaf. The elephant runs from the ram, the lion from the cockerel, whales from the crackle of beans being crushed, and the tiger from the sound of a drum. One could say more than this; but so as not to seem to dwell on it longer than we should: if the same things are unpleasant to some and pleasant to others, and pleasant and unpleasant lie in impressions, then animals get different impressions from the underlying things."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d4951a92fcbd47c298111676dd745c08","ordinal":59,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.59","text":"\n\nAnd if the same things appear dissimilar owing to the variety of animals, we will be able to say how the underlying thing is observed by us, but we will suspend judgment about how it is in its nature. For we will not be able to decide between our own impressions and those of the other animals ourselves. We are part of the disagreement, and so we will need someone to decide it rather than being able to judge it ourselves."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-46d7fd286a24472b8bd50d66f9bc0b2d","ordinal":60,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.60","text":"\n\nBesides, we cannot give our own impressions preference over those that arise in non-rational animals, either without proof or with proof. Quite apart from the fact that there is perhaps no such thing as proof, as we will point out, the so-called proof itself will either appear to us or not appear to us. If it does not appear, we will not put it forward with any confidence. If it does appear to us, then—since what is under investigation is what appears to animals, and the proof appears to us, who are animals—the proof itself will be under investigation as to whether it is true, insofar as it appears."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-2da27967d57b4a9fa7ba60b56700a06e","ordinal":61,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.61","text":"\n\nBut it is absurd to try to establish what is under investigation by means of what is under investigation. The same thing would then be both convincing and unconvincing, which is impossible: convincing insofar as it purports to prove, unconvincing insofar as it is being proved. So we will have no proof by which to give our own impressions preference over those arising in the so-called non-rational animals. If, then, impressions differ owing to the variety of animals, and it is impossible to decide among them, we must suspend judgment about external objects."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-269f4dc1cf0a4adfac11e006c414bd19","ordinal":62,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.62","text":"\n\nFor good measure, we also compare the so-called non-rational animals with human beings with respect to impressions. After our serious arguments, we don’t refuse to have some fun at the expense of the dogmatists, puffed up and self-praising as they are. Our own people usually compare the non-rational animals as a whole with human beings."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9acc1fd34a7d4f438880305acd89d1fe","ordinal":63,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.63","text":"\n\nBut the dogmatists quibble and say the comparison is unfair. So, with plenty to spare and to make the joke even better, we will rest the argument on a single animal—the dog, say, if you like, which is thought to be the lowliest of all. For we will find that even so the animals we are discussing are not inferior to us as regards the credibility of what appears to them."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-112f73e7f00c4ba19e1bd8e1b797049f","ordinal":64,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.64","text":"\n\nNow, the dogmatists agree that this animal surpasses us in perception. It perceives by smell better than we do, using it to track down wild animals it cannot see; it sees them with its eyes more quickly than we do; and its hearing is keen."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4086633f6d5347f19d1355f0c63f87d4","ordinal":65,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.65","text":"\n\nSo let us come to reason. One kind of reason is internal, the other expressed in speech. Let us look first at internal reason. According to the dogmatists who at present most oppose us, the Stoics, internal reason seems to turn on these things: choosing what is one’s own and avoiding what is alien; knowing the arts that contribute to this; and grasping the excellences that belong to one’s own nature ⟨and⟩ those concerned with feelings.\n〔1〕 “Reason” translates logos, which also means “speech”: the Stoics distinguished reason as inner thought from reason uttered in speech."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-02bdcf3682b34af3a9c83555ed231baa","ordinal":66,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.66","text":"\n\nNow, the dog—the animal we decided to rest the argument on, for the sake of example—chooses what is its own and avoids what is harmful: it goes after food and backs away when the whip is raised. It also has an art for procuring what is its own—namely, hunting."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-96de7c9674934263b99ee3f578b966f9","ordinal":67,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.67","text":"\n\nNor is it without excellence. Justice, at any rate, is giving each their due. And since the dog fawns on and guards those who are its own and treat it well, and wards off those who are not its own and do wrong, it could hardly be said to lack justice."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-65718492025e4544933be3dd2dfb8104","ordinal":68,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.68","text":"\n\nBut if it has justice, then, since the excellences entail one another, it also has the other excellences—the ones the wise say most human beings do not have. We see, too, that it is brave in warding people off, and intelligent. Homer testified to this as well: he portrayed Odysseus as unrecognized by all the people of his household and recognized by Argus alone. The dog was neither deceived by the change in the man’s body nor did it lose hold of its apprehensive impression—which it evidently had to a greater degree than the human beings.\n〔1〕 The “apprehensive impression” is the Stoic criterion of truth: an impression so clear that it could only come from the thing it represents. Argus is Odysseus’ old dog in Odyssey 17."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-67b141ffde954ef29402204d5dd29905","ordinal":69,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.69","text":"\n\nAccording to Chrysippus, who is especially hostile to non-rational animals, the dog even shares in their celebrated dialectic. At any rate, the man just mentioned says that the dog applies the fifth indemonstrable with more than two alternatives. This happens when it comes to a place where three roads meet, sniffs at the two roads its quarry did not take, and rushes straight off down the third without even sniffing it. For, says the venerable man, the dog is implicitly reasoning like this: “The animal went either this way, or this way, or this way; but not this way, and not this way;\n〔1〕 The indemonstrables are the five basic argument forms of Stoic logic, from which all others were derived; the fifth runs “Either A or B; not A; therefore B.” Chrysippus (c. 280–207 BC) was the third head of the Stoic school."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a379c4a3f9fc4dc9b0c9fca032148f7e","ordinal":70,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.70","text":"\n\ntherefore this way.” What is more, the dog is aware of its own feelings and able to relieve them. When a thorn has stuck in it, it sets about removing it by rubbing its foot on the ground and with its teeth. And if it has a wound somewhere, then, since dirty wounds are hard to cure while clean ones heal easily, it gently wipes away the pus that forms."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-721ed47648df46bea31da91a3a1e3206","ordinal":71,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.71","text":"\n\nIt also observes Hippocrates’ rule very well. Since rest is the cure for a foot, if it ever gets a wound in its foot it holds the foot up and keeps it as undisturbed as it can. And when it is troubled by humors that don’t agree with it, it eats grass, vomits up what disagrees with it along with the grass, and gets well."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-63ab4670fe514a2fbbf6f0e6d7a69f90","ordinal":72,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.72","text":"\n\nThe animal we rested our argument on for the sake of example has, then, evidently chosen what is its own and avoided what is troublesome; it has an art for procuring what is its own; it is aware of its own feelings and able to relieve them; and it is not without excellence. Since the perfection of internal reason lies in these things, the dog would to that extent be perfect. That, I think, is why some philosophers have dignified themselves with the name of this animal.\n〔1〕 The Cynics, whose name comes from the Greek kyōn, “dog.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e3bc8d84aaf34f05bdb44ea010175ccf","ordinal":73,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.73","text":"\n\nAs for reason expressed in speech, there is no need to investigate it for the present. Even some of the dogmatists have rejected it as a hindrance to acquiring excellence, which is why they practiced silence during the period of their instruction. Besides, if a human being were mute, say, no one would call him non-rational. But let us pass over these points too. We do in fact see the animals we are discussing uttering even human sounds—jays, for instance, and some others.\n〔1〕 The Pythagoreans reportedly imposed a long period of silence on their pupils."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-157868f79842465d85056c03f84563f4","ordinal":74,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.74","text":"\n\nLet us set that aside as well. Even if we do not understand the sounds of the so-called non-rational animals, it is not at all unlikely that they converse and we simply fail to understand. After all, when we hear the speech of foreigners, we don’t understand it, and it seems to us to be all one sound."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-268b656a69da46e49c9ae9dfd9085a01","ordinal":75,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.75","text":"\n\nAnd we hear dogs utter one sound when they are warding someone off, another when they howl, another when they are beaten, and a different one when they fawn. In general, anyone who looked closely into this would find great variation in the voice of this and the other animals in different circumstances. So for these reasons it might reasonably be said that the so-called non-rational animals also share in reason expressed in speech."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3b73b25c460e4cfb8a70b1428faedc94","ordinal":76,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.76","text":"\n\nAnd if they are inferior to human beings neither in the precision of their senses, nor in internal reason, nor—to say it for good measure—in reason expressed in speech, then they would be no less credible than we are as regards their impressions."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-28a141f041114a4295ddbcccc9feeb7a","ordinal":77,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.77","text":"\n\nPerhaps we could show this by resting the argument on each of the non-rational animals in turn. Who, for instance, would deny that birds excel in shrewdness and use reason expressed in speech? They know not only the present but also the future, and they reveal it in advance to those who can understand them, both through other signs and by announcing it with their voices."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-937f9546a4aa42adb3f2d2812bef3fad","ordinal":78,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.78","text":"\n\nAs I indicated before, I made this comparison for good measure, having already shown sufficiently, I think, [earlier] that we cannot give our own impressions preference over those arising in non-rational animals. But if the non-rational animals are no less credible than we are in judging impressions, and different impressions arise owing to the variation among animals, then I will be able to say how each of the underlying things appears to me; but because of what has been said, I will be forced to suspend judgment about how it is in its nature."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a8ac18dcbb98449f818518909e9a4a34","ordinal":79,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.79","text":"\n\nSuch, then, is the first mode of suspension. The second, we said, is the one from the differences among human beings. For even if one were to grant, for the sake of argument, that human beings are more credible than non-rational animals, we will find that suspension of judgment is brought in as far as our own differences go. A human being is said to be composed of two things, soul and body, and we differ from one another in both. In body, for example, we differ in our shapes and in our idiosyncrasies."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5a688c251210413b900271cf587588d4","ordinal":80,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.80","text":"\n\nThe body of a Scythian differs in shape from the body of an Indian, and the variation is produced, so they say, by a different predominance of humors. And impressions too differ with the different predominance of humors, as we established in the first argument. This, indeed, is why there is a great difference among peoples in what external things they choose and avoid: Indians enjoy some things and people in our part of the world others. And enjoying different things is an indication of receiving varying impressions from the underlying things."}]}